    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full build passes green. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded an optional, region-validated `postcode` (4 digits) to owners, keeping the request contract backward-compatible.\n\n**What I changed:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `postcode` property to the `OwnerFields` schema (optional, `pattern: ^[0-9]{4}$`). Since `Owner` inherits `OwnerFields` via `allOf`, this regenerates `postcode` on both the `OwnerFieldsDto` (request) and `OwnerDto` (response), so it's stored-and-returned automatically via MapStruct (which maps it by name \u2014 no mapper edit needed).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 new `postcode` String field/column with getter/setter.\n\n3. **Schema SQL** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added a nullable `postcode VARCHAR(4)` column to the `owners` table. Tests use hsqldb; the others are kept consistent.\n\n4. **`Postcodes.java`** (new helper) \u2014 encapsulates the rule: blank/absent is valid; when present it must be 4 digits and, for a city with a known region (via existing `Localities.forCity`), fall within NSW 2000\u20132099 / VIC 3000\u20133099 / QLD 4000\u20134099. Unknown region accepts any 4-digit code.\n\n5. **`RequireOwnerFields.java`** \u2014 the POST pipeline's manual validation step now rejects an invalid postcode with a 400 (`RequiredFieldsException(\"postcode\")`), matching the existing error contract. This is where validation belongs because the POST path validates manually rather than via `@Valid`.\n\n6. **`ApplyOwner.java`** \u2014 copies `postcode` on PUT updates for consistency (the PUT path enforces the 4-digit pattern via its `@Valid` step against the regenerated DTO).\n\n**Tests:** all 3 new acceptance tests pass (`coreStoresValidPostcode`, `coreAcceptsOwnerWithoutPostcode`, `errorRejectsMalformedPostcode`), and the full `./mvnw test` suite is green.",
